  "Number": 5,
  "Question": "Let \\(d\\) and \\(e\\) denote the solutions of \\(2x^2 + 3x - 5 = 0.\\) What is the value of \\((d - 1)(e - 1)?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-\\dfrac{5}{2}\\)",
    "B": "\\(0\\)",
    "C": "\\(3\\)",
    "D": "\\(5\\)",
    "E": "\\(6\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "Because the requested product contains \\(d-1\\) and \\(e-1\\), it is useful to check whether \\(1\\) is a solution of the equation. Substituting \\(x=1\\) gives\n\\[\n2(1)^2+3(1)-5=2+3-5=0.\n\\]\nThus one of the two solutions \\(d\\) and \\(e\\) equals \\(1\\). The corresponding factor in \\((d-1)(e-1)\\) is zero, so the product is \\(\\boxed{0}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
